Title: "Axiom Systems and Size"
Id: "4183ca80-b7a4-47be-9619-0f46dafc8553"


Text: "The elementary constructors do not by themselves constitute the usual
       Zermelo--Fraenkel theory. Modern set theory also controls how formulas
       select members of an existing set, how functional assignments produce new
       sets, and how membership chains behave. These principles are stated as
       schemes because one axiom is supplied for every suitable formula in the
       language."
Id: "ddb95a61-dddb-4c5a-bb15-430be7091d5a"


SectionTitle: "Separation and Replacement"
Id: "b04301f4-2e38-4797-b6cd-626d4be8bcb7"


TextAxiom: "Axiom schema of separation. For every set $A$ and every formula
            $\varphi(x)$, with any fixed parameters, there exists a set
            $B=\{x\in A: \varphi(x)\}$. Thus a property may carve a subset from
            a previously given set. The ambient set $A$ is indispensable:
            separation does not assert the existence of an unrestricted set
            $\{x:\varphi(x)\}$."
Id: "5077fbc7-a5c8-4d60-b1a7-a9d65097f003"


TextAxiom: "Axiom schema of replacement. Suppose a formula $\varphi(x,y)$ is
            functional on a set $A$: for each $x\in A$ there is exactly one $y$
            such that $\varphi(x,y)$. Then the range
            $\{y:\exists x\in A\,\varphi(x,y)\}$ is a set. Replacement permits a
            set-sized family of objects to be collected even when no common
            ambient set for those objects was known in advance."
Id: "6e09ea04-f1dd-41c0-a0f5-4c962cd819ac"


Text: "Separation selects members already lying in one set; replacement sends
       members of a set to uniquely determined outputs. The distinction becomes
       important in transfinite constructions. Replacement ensures that the
       values obtained at all earlier stages still form a set to which union or
       another constructor can be applied."
Id: "1cbdad45-8bc2-4388-8a12-19b187499517"


SectionTitle: "Foundation and Choice"
Id: "960e9966-e880-421a-afa0-c8c753476a7c"


TextAxiom: "Axiom of foundation. Every nonempty set $A$ has an element $a$
            disjoint from $A$. Equivalently in the presence of the other ZF
            axioms, there is no infinite descending membership chain
            $x_0\ni x_1\ni x_2\ni\cdots$. Foundation supports proofs and
            definitions by membership recursion and excludes self-membership
            phenomena such as $x\in x$."
Id: "cc3fc1c3-8ea9-45a6-874c-9215525952f4"


TextAxiom: "Axiom of choice. For every set $I$ and every family $(A_i)_{i\in I}$
            of nonempty sets, there exists a function $f$ on $I$ such that
            $f(i)\in A_i$ for every $i\in I$. The assertion is substantive when
            the family is infinite and no canonical rule for choosing the
            elements is available. ZF together with this axiom is denoted ZFC."
Id: "3f1ec40e-207c-431e-918e-7a8f4f23fa46"


TextTheorem: "Over ZF, the axiom of choice is equivalent to each of the
              following principles: every set can be well ordered; every
              partially ordered set in which each chain has an upper bound has a
              maximal element (Zorn's lemma); and every surjective function
              admits a right inverse. The equivalences allow choice to enter a
              proof in the form best adapted to its subject."
Id: "694788bb-a939-406b-8a3f-62730d8d0399"


SectionTitle: "Cardinality and Cantor's Theorem"
Id: "ce8978c6-895a-4528-bc27-a1796c5fc751"


TextDefinition: "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the same cardinality if there exists
                 a bijection $A\to B$. We write $|A|\leq|B|$ when there exists
                 an injection $A\to B$. A set is finite if it is bijective with
                 $\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$ for some natural number $n$; it is countably
                 infinite if it is bijective with $\mathbb{N}$, and countable if
                 it is finite or countably infinite."
Id: "5ba55513-d2b3-458e-afd9-2fbd7507d47f"


TextTheorem: "Cantor--Schroeder--Bernstein theorem. If there is an injection
              $A\to B$ and an injection $B\to A$, then there is a bijection
              $A\to B$. Consequently mutual cardinal inequalities imply equal
              cardinality without requiring either injection to be surjective."
Id: "5ad0453f-1203-4467-949b-4c8a8697b5b3"


TextTheorem: "Cantor's theorem. For every set $A$, no function
              $f:A\to\mathcal{P}(A)$ is surjective. Indeed, the diagonal set
              $D=\{a\in A:a\notin f(a)\}$ differs from $f(a)$ at the element $a$
              for every $a\in A$. Hence $|A|<|\mathcal{P}(A)|$, and there is no
              largest cardinality."
Id: "83a30248-2172-45d8-934a-e7a54be66b12"


Text: "Cantor's diagonal argument illustrates the foundational discipline of the
       chapter. The set $D$ exists by separation from $A$, not by unrestricted
       comprehension. Its definition then converts the assumption that $f$ lists
       every subset of $A$ into the contradiction $d\in D\iff d\notin D$ at a
       purported index $d$ for $D$."
Id: "8d163eb8-a148-43d1-80b3-38c54e51e278"
